server : keep the prompt cache on disk, one file per prompt (--cache-disk)

Ollama-local, applies on top of 001-llama-cpp-hooks.patch (the former 002-server-slot-checkpoints
landed upstream as #26004 and is part of b11669). With --cache-disk DIR the
prompt cache writes every entry to its own file under DIR and keeps only its tokens in
memory. Entries survive a restart (the directory is indexed at load), are not
size-limited, and are removed only when a newer prompt contains them. In disk mode a
slot is saved before it is reused when more than a ubatch of it would be lost, or when
a cached prompt matches the task better, so a new conversation sharing only the system
prompt no longer discards the previous one.

The files of entries a new one makes obsolete are removed only after the new file is
published, so a process killed during that write keeps them. On memories that cannot be
truncated (recurrent / hybrid / SWA) an entry the new prompt contains is only obsolete
when the new prompt holds a checkpoint within a ubatch of its end.

Also, for every mode: the end of a leading system message is checkpointed like a user
message, and the earliest checkpoint of a slot is never the one evicted to make room.
A prompt truncated from the front (older messages dropped to fit the context) shares
only the system prompt with the slot, and resumes from that checkpoint instead of
re-processing everything.

